purpose: The document tree. Nodes, elements, text; ownership and traversal.

# dom depends on util only. Style, layout and script all depend on dom, so a
# dependency the other way would put the whole engine below the tree.
#
# A constraint from outside this module, recorded here because this is where it
# will be tripped over: **`Node::Remove` has no callers, and giving it one is
# not a small change.** The DOM binding layer hands script a JavaScript object
# holding a raw `Node*`. That is safe only because nothing frees a node before
# its document -- Remove would, and nothing calls it. The first caller makes a
# wrapper able to outlive its node, which is a use-after-free reachable from a
# page. Whoever adds that caller owns fixing it in the same commit;
# docs/adr/0008-dom-bindings.md says what the two acceptable fixes are.
allow: util

public: Node.h FlatTree.h Namespaces.h

extern:

# Raised from 800 for declarative shadow DOM (ADR 0019 + HTML §13.2.6.4.4).
# What arrived is one enum and one free function family, and neither is a new
# subject: `ShadowFlags` is the state a shadow root already had spread across
# two bools, and `SerializeNode`/`SerializeFragment` are the serializer that was
# already here, now written once instead of six times. The file got longer
# because the *arguments* got longer -- most of the added lines are the comments
# saying why serializing a shadow tree needs the page to ask twice.
# 841 after `shadowrootadoptedstylesheets`: three accessors and the paragraph
# saying why the specifiers are kept as text and *not* resolved -- which is the
# obligation the next session inherits, so it is the wrong comment to shorten.
# 863 after two worktrees met in a merge: declarative shadow DOM's
# `AttachShadow(ShadowFlags)` and ADR 0027's `NestedDocument` landed against the
# same base, and both are on `Element`. Neither can be split out the way an
# installer can -- a *member* cannot leave a class's header -- and the second is
# the one to watch: a raw `Document*` this module does not own, whose whole
# safety argument is the paragraph above it. Shortening that paragraph to save
# two lines would be deleting the reason the pointer is allowed to exist.
# Raised by four for `ElementState::Indeterminate` and the sentence saying what makes it different
# from every other bit here: it is IDL-only state with no attribute behind it, so unlike `:checked`
# it cannot be derived from the tree and has to be stored.
max_tu_lines: 875

# Twenty-one, for `IsDocumentFragment`. It sits beside IsElement and IsText
# for the same reason those do: the kind is a switch a caller would
# otherwise write out, and insertion has to ask this question on every
# append -- a fragment inserts its children rather than itself.
# Grew for NoteMutation and its comment -- protected, so the public surface did
# not move. It is here rather than at the callers above dom because *missing a
# call* is the failure mode: anything derived from the tree goes stale silently
# and a page reads a rectangle describing the page as it was.
# Grew for ReleaseFocusWithin and its comment -- protected, beside NoteMutation
# and there for the same reason: the document's focus is a raw Element* and
# *missing a call* is the failure mode. Script removing the focused element
# would otherwise leave the next key routed at a node no longer in the tree.
# The twenty-second public method is ConnectedDocument, and it is a
# deduplication rather than a widening: NoteMutation and ReleaseFocusWithin both
# walked to the root and both had to know what a Document is, and the selector
# matcher needs the same walk to answer `:focus` from the one copy of focus.
# Three copies of a loop is how one of them ends up stopping at the wrong node.
# It was called OwnerDocument until 2026-08-11, and the rename is the point: it
# is not what a page calls `ownerDocument`, and reading one for the other is a
# node claiming to be in a document it was only *made* by.
# The third member is the *node document* -- what `ownerDocument` answers.
# Stored rather than walked, and the two are different facts: the walk answers
# "which rendered tree is this in", which is null for a node script made and has
# not inserted, while the DOM assigns a node document at *creation* and it
# survives detachment. No walk can derive it, which is why this is the one
# pointer the module's own comment argued against and now carries. It costs
# 8 bytes on every node; what it buys is `document.createElement` on an inert
# document answering that document rather than the page on screen.
budget: Node     header_lines=185 public_methods=25 members=4
# The third member is the element's dynamic state: a bitmask of the states a
# selector can match on that the tree does not otherwise record. ADR 0016 §2
# decided it belongs here rather than in the matcher, and the enum's comment in
# Node.h says which states are stored, which are derived, and why focus is
# deliberately not one of them.
# The fourth member is a `<template>`'s contents, and it is null on every other
# element. It is here rather than in a subclass because both things that make
# elements -- the tree builder and `createElement` -- create them by tag name
# and have nowhere to put a second type. Before it, a template's markup was its
# children: the cascade styled it, layout laid it out and the loader fetched its
# images, which is the opposite of what the element means.
# +6 public methods and +2 members for the namespace an element is in
# (task C4). LocalName/Prefix/Namespace and the three `…NS` attribute
# operations are the surface; `namespace_` and `prefix_length_` are the
# storage, and they sit in padding the two fragment pointers already left --
# measured, sizeof(Element) is 136 either way, so an element that remembers its
# namespace is the same size as one that did not. The pair is what lets `tagName`, `localName`, `prefix` and
# `namespaceURI` be four different answers instead of two guesses at one
# field.
# +1 member for ADR 0027's nested browsing contexts: the document inside an
# `<iframe>`. It is a *borrowed* pointer -- the only one on a node in this
# module -- and the two rules that make it safe are written at the member: it is
# set only by `src/engine`, and only when the child is same-origin, so the
# origin check is the pointer's absence rather than a test somebody has to
# remember. `src/engine`'s Page::ClearFrames is the one place it is cleared, and
# it clears before dropping the document it points at.
budget: Element      header_lines=200 public_methods=30 members=10
# +1 AttrVersion / attr_version_: style-cache provenance so an attribute write
# on one host does not flush every other element's cascade (TD-0021).
# The namespace an element or attribute is in, as a counted handle rather than
# a URI string. Its size is the point: four bytes against thirty-two, and an
# integer compare where getElementsByTagNameNS would otherwise do a string one.
# Measured: sizeof(Element) is 136 before and after, because the handle and the
# prefix length landed in padding the two fragment pointers already left;
# sizeof(Attribute) went 64 -> 72. Most of the header is the argument for the
# reference counting, which is what keeps a page's ten million one-off
# namespaces from being a leak.
budget: NamespaceRef header_lines=55 public_methods=13 members=1
# An attribute's qualified name, value, namespace and prefix length. Four
# members because the last two are what make `localName`, `prefix` and
# `namespaceURI` three different answers instead of one guess.
budget: Attribute    header_lines=30 public_methods=2  members=4
# Two members and 40 header lines: the data, and whether this Text is a
# CDATASection. The second is a flag rather than a seventh `Node::Kind` on
# purpose -- a CDATASection derives from Text in the DOM and every algorithm
# that measures a length, splits at an offset or walks tree order wants the
# same answer for both, so a new Kind would mean editing a dozen exhaustive
# switches to say "text" again and shipping whichever one was missed as a
# silently wrong answer. `nodeType` and `nodeName` are the only two callers.
budget: Text         header_lines=40 public_methods=9  members=2
budget: Comment      header_lines=25 public_methods=6  members=1
# Three members: the name plus the public and system ids. Kept rather than
# dropped because they are what a doctype *is* to `createDocumentType`, and
# because they are the input to the quirks-mode decision the tokenizer already
# makes.
budget: DocumentType header_lines=40 public_methods=8  members=3
# A target and its data. Two members and not one: `nodeName` on one of these is
# the target, which is the whole reason it is not a Comment.
budget: ProcessingInstruction header_lines=40 public_methods=8 members=2
# The second member is the mutation version: how many times anything in this
# tree has changed. A document-level counter rather than a per-node bit, because
# its readers are caches of the whole tree -- the box tree today, ADR 0016's
# invalidation index next -- and a bit each of them cleared would hide a change
# from the next one to look.
# The third member is the focus: which element has it, and whether the keyboard
# put it there. On the document because that is what focus *is* (ADR 0017 §4),
# and because the two things that read it -- the engine, which routes every key
# to it, and the binding layer, which reports it as `document.activeElement` --
# are in different modules. A copy in either would be the pair that disagrees
# about where the next keystroke goes. One member and not two, for the reason
# Page::LayoutState is one: they are two facts about the same thing.
# +1 member for ADR 0017's user activation, read by ADR 0028 §1's autoplay refusal. On the
# document because that is its scope: an interaction with one document does not license
# another.
# +1 structure_version_: tree-shape half of MutationVersion for TD-0021's style
# cache. Attribute writes leave it alone; insert/remove bump both.
# +1 member for the document's *type* -- HTML or XML -- which arrived with
# `DOMParser` (task C9). It is a bit rather than a MIME string on purpose: the
# two things in the tree that branch on it are `createElement`'s case folding
# and `tagName`'s upper-casing, and both want the bit. The exact content type a
# page reads back (`text/xml` against `image/svg+xml`) is not a tree fact and
# stays on the binding layer's wrapper, where the rest of "how was this object
# made" already lives.
budget: Document     header_lines=130 public_methods=24 members=7
# A fragment, plus the one link back from a shadow root to its host. Deliberately
# not the fragment's parent: a shadow root with a parent would be reachable by
# every ordinary tree walk, and being unreachable that way is the whole point of
# it. ADR 0019 §2.
# members=2: `template_content_` distinguishes `<template>.content` from ShadyDOM
# roots and `createDocumentFragment()` — all three are host-less fragments.
#
# +5 public methods and **no new member** for declarative shadow DOM. The five
# are `Flags`/`SetFlags`/`SetShadowFlag` and the six one-line predicates over
# them, and the member count did not move because `template_content_` folded
# *into* the mask: a bool and a bitmask are one field, and the two questions --
# "is this template contents" and "how was this root attached" -- are asked of
# the same three kinds of parentless fragment.
#
# The state landed here rather than on Element deliberately. Every one of these
# is a fact about the *root*: `attachShadow` takes them, `getHTML` reports them,
# and an element with no root has no opinion on any of them. Putting them on
# Element would also have needed a budget raise there, where this needed none --
# `shadow_open_` moved off Element in the same change, so Element went 9 members
# to 8 while gaining the whole feature.
#
# +3 public methods and **+1 member** for `shadowrootadoptedstylesheets`, kept
# verbatim so `getHTML` can hand back what the author wrote. The member is a
# string and the fourth here; it is not derivable from `adopted_style_sheets_`,
# which holds resolved sheets and is empty because resolving a specifier needs
# an import map this browser has no module loader for. Absent and
# authored-empty are different documents, which is why the flag beside it
# exists rather than testing the string.
budget: DocumentFragment header_lines=105 public_methods=18 members=4
# The bitmask itself. A budget so that later growth is visible, which is the
# point of the mechanism: the next bit added to a shadow root shows up in a diff
# against this line rather than disappearing into a header.
budget: ShadowFlags header_lines=45 public_methods=0 members=0
